# 0023. An ACP adapter for other coding agents

- Status: Accepted (owner, 2026-11-25; revised after Codex's critique the same day)
- Amends: [0011](0011-hot-model-registry.adr) (a third adapter kind)
- Date: 2026-11-25

[0004](0004-cli-agent-adapters.adr) planned an `acp` adapter "once ACP v2 settles". This
ADR decides what it is for. Neither Claude Code (2.1.332) nor Codex (0.172.0) speaks ACP
natively, and both already have direct adapters, so ACP's value is adding **other** agents
(Gemini CLI, and any agent with an ACP mode) without code per agent.

## Technical details
- **Registry** (amends 0011): a third `kind = "acp"`, dispatched explicitly (today every
  non-CLI participant goes down the OpenAI path in `App::start`):
  ```toml
  [[model]]
  id = "gemini"
  kind = "acp"
  cmd = "gemini"
  args = ["--experimental-acp"]
  read_only_args = ["…"]   # required: this agent's verified read-only mode
  ```
  No agent is verified yet: Gemini CLI's `--sandbox`, for one, still allows writes to its
  workspace, so it does not qualify. Each agent's read-only invocation is checked for a
  pinned version before its entry is supported, and documented with its fixture.
  The identity fingerprint covers `cmd`, `args` and `read_only_args`, so changing how the
  agent is launched counts as a new destination (history and sessions don't carry over).
- **Wire version: ACP v2 only** (what 0004 waited for). `initialize` negotiates the
  protocol version and capabilities; an agent that only speaks v1 is refused with that
  reason. Sessions: `session/new`, then `session/resume` for every later turn (the
  process is reaped after each turn, see below). An agent without the session capability,
  which in v2 includes resume, is refused: a reviewer that forgets each turn is no use.
- **A turn's end**: in v2 the `session/prompt` request is acknowledged before the turn
  finishes, so the acknowledgement is never treated as the end. The turn ends on the
  agent's terminal foreground `state_update` (its exact shape pinned by the recorded
  fixture), after which
  the process is reaped as for the CLIs ([0004](0004-cli-agent-adapters.adr)); `Esc` sends
  `session/cancel`, then kills the process group after the grace period.
- **Read-only, and only as a reviewer** (v1 of this adapter): an ACP agent is never the
  `writer` and never writable. A client cannot stop an arbitrary executable from doing
  its own file or shell work, so admission needs both: the entry's `read_only_args`
  (the agent's own verified read-only mode; an entry without it is refused) and its own
  worktree ([0016](0016-worktree-per-agent.adr)) as working folder. Outside git, ACP
  agents are refused.
- **Permission requests** (`session/request_permission`) are answered with one of the
  rejection options the agent offered, or the protocol's `cancelled` outcome, never a
  free-form "deny"; every one is logged as an `approval` event (0018) with `who:
  read-only`.
- **Client methods**: v2 has no client `fs/*` or terminal methods; anything the agent
  sends that roundtable does not implement gets a JSON-RPC method-not-found error.
- **`ask`** ([0007](0007-model-to-model-communication.adr)): ACP agents can be asked, but in
  this version get no `ask` tool themselves: a scope decision, to be added later by
  passing the roundtable server, with a fresh token, in each turn's `mcpServers` (v2's
  `session/resume` accepts the full current list).
- **`/fork`** ([0019](0019-fork.adr), which notes this exception): ACP has no native fork;
  in a fork an ACP agent starts a new session seeded only with what it was sent before
  under its current identity, never other participants' exchanges.
- **Versioning**: the crate is pinned, and each supported agent gets a recorded,
  sanitized fixture of a full turn and a cancel, as for the CLIs.
- **Not in scope**: switching Claude or Codex to ACP. Their JSON adapters stay, per 0004.
- Consequences: one adapter brings other agents in as reviewers. Costs: a large protocol
  surface to map and test, each agent implements ACP its own way, and it needs 0016
  first; real use needs at least one agent the owner wants at the table.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: `agent-client-protocol` crate (5.6.1 at writing), JSON-RPC over stdio
- Alternatives:
  - One hand-written adapter per extra agent (as for Claude and Codex): precise, but code
    for each new agent.
  - Wait: no agent beyond Claude and Codex is planned today; the owner may prefer to keep
    this Proposed until one is.
